  "title": "Distinct real roots",
  "prompt": "Find the general solution of $y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0$.",
  "answer_format": "y = ... in terms of x. Use C (or C1, C2) for arbitrary constants.",
  "difficulty": 1,
  "tags": [
    "second order",
    "constant coefficients"
  ],
  "data": {
    "ode": "y'' - 3y' + 2y = 0"
  },
  "answer": "y = C1 e^x + C2 e^(2x)",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Try y = e^(rx): you get the characteristic equation r² − 3r + 2 = 0."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Characteristic equation: $r^2 - 3r + 2 = (r - 1)(r - 2) = 0$, so $r = 1, 2$.",
    "$y = C_1 e^{x} + C_2 e^{2x}$."
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "openstax-calc3",
    "section": "7.1 Second-Order Linear Equations",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
